Barnaby was not your average house cat.

While the other cats in the neighborhood were content to nap in sunbeams, chase dust motes, and stare judgmentally at birds through the window, Barnaby’s emerald eyes were always fixed on the horizon. He was a ginger tabby with a white chest, a crooked tail, and a spirit that felt far too large for the small suburban house he called home.

His humans called him “the explorer,” though they usually said it with a sigh when he tracked mud across the kitchen floor or knocked over a potted fern while chasing a moth. They thought he was merely mischievous. They did not understand that Barnaby was not causing trouble. He was gathering intelligence.

Every morning, he sat by the back door and watched the world beyond the fence. The wind carried scents from places he had never been: damp earth, pine needles, warm asphalt, and something else—something wild and electric that made his whiskers twitch. He dreamed of the Great Beyond, the mysterious land that lay past the garden, past the street, and beyond the tall iron gate at the end of the lane.

One crisp autumn evening, the back door was left slightly ajar.

To Barnaby, it was not an accident. It was a summons.

He rose from his cushion, stretched until his spine curved like a bow, and padded silently across the kitchen floor. The house was quiet. His humans were in the living room, watching the glowing rectangle they called television. The door stood open just enough for him to slip through, and beyond it, the garden waited under a pale moon.

The garden was familiar, yet at night it became something else entirely. The rose bushes loomed like thorny fortresses. The garden hose lay coiled in the shadows like a sleeping serpent. The old oak tree at the far end of the yard rose above everything else, its branches black against the sky. Barnaby had always avoided the tree. It was too tall, too wild, too full of sounds he could not explain.

Tonight, however, the tree called to him.

He moved through the grass with silent precision, his paws barely disturbing the dew. A cricket stopped chirping as he passed. A moth fluttered past his nose, and he almost leaped after it, but he resisted. This was not a game. This was an expedition.

When he reached the base of the oak, he paused. The bark was rough beneath his claws, and the branches twisted upward like the fingers of an ancient giant. From somewhere high above came a sound so faint he might have imagined it.

A tiny, trembling cry.

Barnaby’s ears flattened. He looked up.

There, tangled in a nest of twigs and leaves, was a small gray kitten. Its fur was matted, its eyes wide with fear, and one of its hind legs was caught in a loop of old fishing line someone had carelessly left behind. The kitten struggled weakly, letting out another pitiful squeak.

Barnaby did not think. He climbed.

The branches scraped against his belly, and the wind tugged at his fur. He climbed higher than he ever had before, his claws digging into the bark, his heart pounding. The world below grew smaller. The rooftops of the neighborhood looked like toy houses. The streetlights glowed like fallen stars.

When he reached the branch where the kitten was trapped, he approached slowly. The kitten hissed, then whimpered.

Barnaby lowered his head and sniffed the line. It was thin but strong. He bit down gently, pulling with his teeth. The line tightened around the kitten’s leg, and the kitten cried out. Barnaby paused, steadying himself. He tried again, this time pulling sideways instead of straight. The knot loosened.

With one final tug, the line came free.

The kitten collapsed onto the branch, trembling. Barnaby nudged it gently with his nose. The kitten looked up at him, eyes shining with fear and gratitude.

Barnaby meowed softly. *We must go down.*

The descent was harder than the climb. The kitten was weak, and the branches were slick
